<BaseLayout title="How PDD works" description="Reconstruct current engineering judgment from principle files, agent indexes, code citations, reviews, and CLI checks." schemaType="AboutPage" section="The method" toc={toc} previous={{ title: 'Set up your repository', href: '/blueprint' }} next={{ title: 'CLI', href: '/cli' }}>
  <h1>How PDD works</h1>
  <p class="doc-lead">A new contributor can reconstruct the current judgment without a context handoff.</p>
  <p>PDD puts the rule and its active dependencies in the repository. Developers and agents use that evidence to make decisions.</p>
  <p>The method gives contributors the same basis for judgment. It can support several valid implementations.</p>

  <h2 id="the-decision-system">The decision system</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>A principle file states the judgment.</strong> It includes the benefit, costly failure, concrete effects, exceptions, and version history.</li>
    <li><strong>The agent index routes each contributor.</strong> Each entry in <code>AGENTS.md</code> links a current token to the full rule.</li>
    <li><strong>Comments cite local dependencies.</strong> A versioned token connects the code to the meaning that its author used.</li>
    <li><strong>Reviews cite current rules.</strong> A “working as designed” decision names the rule that justifies the behavior.</li>
    <li><strong>The CLI checks the connections.</strong> It reports missing entries, invalid citations, and old versions.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><a href="/example">The complete example</a> follows one permission rule through all five parts.</p>

  <h2 id="write-a-principle">Write a principle</h2>
  <p>A principle preserves judgment that must guide future decisions. A repeated correction supplies evidence, but it does not automatically become a principle.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it answers</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Rule</td><td>What choice must a contributor make?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Benefit</td><td>What work, complexity, or risk does this choice remove?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Costly failure</td><td>What repeated behavior earned this rule?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Effects</td><td>What changes in code, tests, and reviews?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>Which narrow cases need a different choice?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>History</td><td>When and why did its meaning change?</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>A useful rule changes an actual decision. For example, use-time validation stops an agent from adding coordination code for every timing gap.</p>
  <p>Use short sentences and one name for each concept. Define necessary technical terms before they carry the explanation.</p>

  <h2 id="adopt-a-principle">Adopt a principle</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Gather evidence from repeated problems or deliberate tradeoffs.</li>
    <li>Read every current principle.</li>
    <li>If a current rule already states the judgment, use that rule.</li>
    <li>If the proposal changes an existing rule’s judgment, advance that rule.</li>
    <li>If the judgment governs an independent decision, add a token.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The <a href="/blueprint#principle-skill">PDD principle skill</a> applies this procedure in supported coding harnesses.</p>
  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Keep the registry small.</strong>Remove a principle that no longer affects a decision. Version control preserves its history.</p></div>

  <h2 id="change-a-principle">Change a principle</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Change the principle before dependent content or code.</li>
    <li>If its meaning changes, increment the version.</li>
    <li>Run <code>pdd check</code> to find old citations.</li>
    <li>Review each dependent site against the new rule.</li>
    <li>Update each citation after its review.</li>
    <li>Include the principle and dependent changes in the same pull request.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>A spelling correction does not change the rule’s meaning. A new requirement or exception can change it.</p>

  <h2 id="how-pdd-fits">How PDD fits</h2>
  <p>PDD uses existing repository tools. It adds conventions that connect current judgment to its dependencies.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Repository tool</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>AGENTS.md</code></td><td>Routes contributors to applicable instructions and rules.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Skills</td><td>Supply repeatable procedures for a task.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Task plans</td><td>State the work for one change.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tests</td><td>Check the expected behavior.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Architecture records</td><td>Explain the history of an architectural decision.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>PDD principles</td><td>State current judgment and identify code that depends on it.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>The CLI checks definitions and citations. It cannot determine whether a principle is useful or whether an implementation obeys it.</p>
</BaseLayout>
